### Market update - 47% spare capacity

The four ‘Cs’ – the steel advantage 
CAPACITY: The New Zealand structural steel industry is well equipped to cope with a spike in construction demand 
brought on by the Government’s aim to fire up the industry to support New Zealand’s economic recovery in response to 
COVID-19. The industry has proven capacity to deliver on all projects – there is no need for procurers to go offshore to 
satisfy demand. 
SCNZ’s latest quarterly fabricator forward-workload survey indicates there is significant spare industry capacity for the year 
ending June 2020 – estimated at 47 per cent, based on a current estimated total capacity of 120,000 tonnes per annum. 
In 2019 the construction sector experienced an unprecedented peak of activity, which our industry managed well. In 
the past 12 months the New Zealand structural steel sector turned approximately 100,000 tonnes of structural steel into 
buildings and bridges through its network of steel distributors, fabricators and erectors. Crucially, our structural steel 
industry is highly collaborative, with fabricators partnering with each other nationwide to ensure a project is delivered to a 
high standard, on time and on budget. 
SCNZ Estimated Fabrication Tonnages (per quarter) 
