URL: https://www.sesoc.org.nz/static/Documents/Conference/2023/SESOC2023-0040-Leach-40-262-Leach-Chloe.pdf
ABSTRACT 
The City Rail Link (CRL) engaged Link Alliance to design and build all new structures on 
Auckland’s $5.5billion underground metro project. 
As one of the four stations comprising the City Rail Link, Te Waihorotiu Station consists of a 
four-storey building above the 20m deep and 370m long underground station, all being 
constructed underneath the busy bus corridor of Albert Street in Auckland’s city centre. The 
City Rail Link is the largest transport infrastructure project ever undertaken in New Zealand, 
and because of the project’s size, importance, and complexity, a full-time Construction Phase 
Services (CPS) team was needed on site to ensure the design was being correctly interpreted 
during the station’s complex construction. 
This paper examines the role of the CPS engineer and the benefits that having an onsite team 
to represent the structural design bring to a project of this scale. This paper will focus on the 
experiences of being part of the Te Waihorotiu Station site CPS team as an emerging 
professional, highlighting the value of this experience at the beginning of one’s career. 
Additionally, this paper will discuss the importance of a strong collaborative relationship 
between the design and construction disciplines, drawing on lessons learned from witnessing 
the real-time construction of previously conceptualized designs to achieve a ‘best-for-project’ 
outcome. 
