Definition

A zero cost collar is an options strategy used to lock in a gain by buying an out-of-the-money (OTM) put and selling a same-priced OTM call.
...
- A zero cost collar is an options strategy that combines selling a call and buying a put to hedge against price volatility.
- This strategy helps limit potential losses while capping earnings, making it useful for protecting substantial stock gains.
- Though termed “costless,” the zero cost collar might incur trading fees and not always achieve a zero net cost due to mismatched premiums.

## What Is a Zero Cost Collar? 

 A zero cost collar protects an investment from large price declines while limiting the gains. It works by buying an out-of-the-money (OTM) put option, which provides downside protection, and selling an OTM call option, which helps offset the cost of the put. Investors use it to protect profits after a stock has risen significantly. A zero cost collar can help investors during volatile markets because it balances risk and reward while reducing costs. 

## Understanding the Mechanics of Zero Cost Collar 

 A zero cost collar strategy involves the outlay of money on one half of the strategy, which offsets the cost incurred by the other half. It is a protective options strategy implemented after your long position in a stock experiences substantial gains. 

 To create this position, you use stock you own, buy a protective put, and sell a covered call. Other names for this strategy include zero cost options, equity risk reversals, and hedge wrappers. 

 

