ttractive long-run return characteristics of the equity market have made large equity allocations a common feature of defined-benefit pension plans. According to international news paper Pensions & Investments, the 1,000 largest defined-benefit plans in the US fund their liabilities with an average asset mix of 60% equi ties. The higher allocation of equity is intended to earn the greatest risk-adjusted returns in the long run. However, pension plans and their corporate sponsors are discovering that equities as an asset class may embody greater near-term and medium-term risk than they can toler ate. A difficult market environment is challenging pension funds and their sponsors as they endeavour to meet asset-return targets. Indeed, many corporate, state, and municipal plans are now underfunded. A low interest-rate environment has further aggravated pension plan funding status. A pension plan owes beneficiaries a series of payments at retirement, typically based on salary and years of service. This liabil ity is quantified as the present value of expected future benefit pay ments. Because of the long-term nature of benefits, the present value is extremely sensitive to changes in the discounting rate. For example, FAS 87 requires US plans to use a market rate based on long-term cor porate bond rates to determine the present value of their pension lia bilities. In 2002, most US pension plans reduced the discount rate used for GAAP valuations from 7.25% to 6.75%.
