A collar is a covered call with a safety net. You keep your 100 shares, sell a call above the market to collect premium, and buy a put below the market to protect against a drawdown. Often structured so the call premium pays for the put, creating a zero-cost collar. You cap your upside at the short-call strike; you cap your downside at the long-put strike. The trade in plain English: "I'll give up the upside I don't expect in exchange for locking out the downside I fear."
...
Three legs, one thesis: you own shares of a stock you plan to hold but can't afford to see fall 20%+. The call pays for the put. If both expire worthless (stock stays between the strikes), you net the small premium difference. If the stock rises past the short call, you sell at the call strike plus keep the initial net premium. If the stock drops past the long put, you exercise (or sell the put) and floor out at `put strike − net premium cost`.
...
The example is illustrative, since strike selection, deltas, and IV change premium pricing. Real-world collars often target 16-delta puts and 20-30-delta calls so the call premium exceeds or equals the put cost.
...
