IntermediateThe Collar Strategy
Last updated: Sun Apr 19 2026TL;DR
A collar is a covered call with a safety net. You keep your 100 shares, sell a call above the market to collect premium, and buy a put below the market to protect against a drawdown. Often structured so the call premium pays for the put, creating a zero-cost collar. You cap your upside at the short-call strike; you cap your downside at the long-put strike. The trade in plain English: "I'll give up the upside I don't expect in exchange for locking out the downside I fear."
What you're actually agreeing to
A collar is three positions on the same underlying, sized identically per 100 shares:
Long 100 shares. The core position you already hold.
Long one out-of-the-money put. Insurance. If the stock crashes, this put gains value and limits your loss to (entry price − put strike) − net premium.
Short one out-of-the-money call. Income. If the stock rallies past the call strike, your shares are called away at that strike. In exchange, you collect premium up front.
Three legs, one thesis: you own shares of a stock you plan to hold but can't afford to see fall 20%+. The call pays for the put. If both expire worthless (stock stays between the strikes), you net the small premium difference. If the stock rises past the short call, you sell at the call strike plus keep the initial net premium. If the stock drops past the long put, you exercise (or sell the put) and floor out at put strike − net premium cost.
The collar works best on stocks you plan to hold for a catalyst (earnings a month out, a product launch, a sector-wide event) where you're moderately bullish but unwilling to stomach a -20% outcome if the catalyst disappoints.
A real example
Today, SPY is at $777.29. Suppose you own 100 shares and you set up a 30-day collar: buy the $740.00 put for $1.80 and sell the $815.00 call for $1.75.
Net premium: $-0.05 per share (= $-5 total for 100 shares, near zero)
Max profit if called away: $3766 (upside capped at the call strike)
Max loss if the stock crashes: $3734 (downside floored at the put strike)
Protection window: below $740.00 you're floored; above $815.00 you're called.
The example is illustrative, since strike selection, deltas, and IV change premium pricing. Real-world collars often target 16-delta puts and 20-30-delta calls so the call premium exceeds or equals the put cost.
The zero-cost collar framingZero-cost collar
The clearest way to understand a collar is the zero-cost collar: pick the short-call strike so the call premium you collect exactly offsets the long-put premium you pay. The insurance is free. The cost is giving up upside above the call strike.
How to structure it:
Pick your protective put strike first. Typical choice: 5-10% below the current price, at roughly 20-30 delta. That's your downside floor.
Read the put premium. That's your insurance cost per share.
Scan the call chain at the same (or slightly earlier) expiration. Find the strike where the call premium ≈ put premium. That's your zero-cost collar short call.
The trade-off is explicit: the tighter the call strike (closer to the money), the more premium it collects, which lets you buy a more protective put strike (closer to the money) while staying zero-cost. A tight zero-cost collar has a narrow profit band. A loose zero-cost collar has a wider profit band but gives up more upside ratio per dollar of downside protected.
Most collar traders settle on a 5% OTM put + the call strike that pays for it (often 5-7% OTM). That gives a ~10-12% profit band before one leg triggers.
When a collar makes sense
You already own 100+ shares of a ticker you plan to hold long-term.
You want protection against a specific downside event, such as earnings, a product launch, or a sector correction, without selling the position.
You're okay capping upside in exchange for the floor. The call's strike is your "I'd take profits here" price.
You manage positions actively and are comfortable with 3-leg tracking (shares + put + call).
Implied volatility is elevated, so the short call collects more premium, which pays for a better put.
When it does NOT make sense
You're bearish. If you genuinely think the stock is going down, sell the shares. A collar is insurance against a loss, not a hedge against a thesis change.
Low-IV, high-uncertainty environment. Low IV makes the short call cheap (you don't get paid enough to collar) while the put remains expensive relative to the call.
You can't tolerate giving up any upside. If your thesis is "this stock doubles in a year," a collar caps you at maybe +10% over the 30-day window. Capping is real opportunity cost.
Tax-locked shares. If the short call gets exercised, you sell the shares, and if they have large unrealized gains, that's a taxable event. Adjust strikes to minimize exercise risk, or don't collar.
Variants
Net-credit collar: if you pick a tighter call strike (closer to money, higher premium) than your put strike, the call premium exceeds the put cost. You net a small credit up front. Payoff: you get paid to set up the protection, at the cost of a narrower profit band.
Net-debit collar: looser call strike, or a more aggressive put (closer to money). You pay a small net debit for stronger downside protection. Best when you expect a specific bad event but want to keep most of the upside if it doesn't materialize.
LEAPS collar: use a long-dated LEAPS put (6-12 months) for the long-term floor, then sell monthly short calls against it. Works for long-term holds where you want continuous downside protection without constantly rolling the put.
Collar vs covered call: same upside profile, different downside. A plain CC leaves your downside fully exposed; a collar adds the long put. If you already run CCs and are worried about a drawdown, adding a put converts your CC into a collar.
Collar vs protective put: same downside profile, different funding. A standalone protective put costs out-of-pocket premium; a collar pays for that put via the short call. Trade-off: the collar gives up upside.
The three things that can go wrong
Stock pins between the strikes. Best case for the collar: you earn the small net premium (if any) plus whatever your shares moved. But "pins" means no drama: if you were paying for insurance that didn't activate, the net debit is dead weight.
Early assignment on the short call. If the short call goes deep ITM near an ex-dividend date and the dividend exceeds the call's extrinsic value, the call buyer will exercise early to capture the dividend. Your shares get called away before you can roll. The fix: avoid selling short calls through ex-dividend dates, or close the call leg before the record date.
Put becomes expensive after a volatility spike. If implied volatility spikes (e.g., the stock drops 10% on unrelated news), your long put gains value, but the short call's IV also spikes, pinching you if you try to close the call to re-collar at new strikes. Wait for IV to mean-revert before adjusting.
