## What Is a Collar? 

 A collar is an options strategy that protects an investment by limiting potential losses and capping potential gains. It involves buying an out-of-the-money (OTM) put option to protect against a price decline and selling an OTM call option to offset the cost of the protection. Investors often use this strategy to manage risk while holding a stock position. Keep reading to learn how collars work, their pros and cons, and examples of how traders use them to protect their investments. 

 

 Investopedia / Sydney Burns 

## How Collar Strategies Work 

 Investors typically use collars if they own a stock whose value is higher than when they bought it, and they are optimistic about the stock going up but unsure of its shorter-term prospects. To protect their gains against a downside move in the stock, they can carry out the collar option strategy. The best outcome for an investor is when the stock’s price matches the set price of the call option they’ve sold right when the option is about to expire. 1 

 The collar strategy combines two methods: buying a put to protect against price drops and selling a call to earn some money upfront. Let’s break this down: 

