An investor may sell a call option at a strike somewhere above today's level, providing the funds to purchase the put option (taken together this is called a collar). One unfortunate reality of options pricing is the "skew". Simply, skew is a concept that causes the following to be true: For a put and call that are equally as far out-of-the-money with the same expiry date, the put will command a higher premium than the call.

