A collar is three positions ... , sized identically per 100 shares:

1. Long 100 shares. The core position you already hold.
2. Long one out-of-the-money put. Insurance. If the stock crashes, this put gains value and limits your loss to `(entry price − put strike) − net premium`.
3. Short one out-of-the-money call. Income. If the stock rallies past the call strike, your shares are called away at that strike. In exchange, you collect premium up front.

Three legs, one thesis: you own shares of a stock you plan to hold but can't afford to see fall 20%+. The call pays for the put. If both expire worthless (stock stays between the strikes), you net the small premium difference. If the stock rises past the short call, you sell at the call strike plus keep the initial net premium. If the stock drops past the long put, you exercise (or sell the put) and floor out at `put strike − net premium cost`.
