A zero-cost collar is two option contracts around stock you own: you buy a protective put that pays out if the stock drops below a chosen floor, and you fund it by selling a call that gives away your gains above a chosen cap. The cash cost nets to zero; the economic cost is the upside you surrender above the cap. A protective put on its own keeps all your upside and instead costs cash premium, typically 3-5% of position value per year for a 1-year contract with the floor 30% below the current price. A third structure, the put spread, keeps the put but cuts its cost by selling a second put lower down, at the price of protection that stops at that lower strike. All three fit a large position in one stock you can't or don't want to sell yet, for tax, lockup, or insider-window reasons. All three work, until they don't.
