A zero cost collar — also called a costless collar, equity risk reversal, or hedge wrapper — is an options strategy applied to an existing long position in a stock. It combines two simultaneous options positions:

1. Buying a put option (out of the money, below current share price) to establish a downside floor
2. Selling a call option (out of the money, above current share price) to generate premium income that offsets the cost of the put

When the premium received from selling the call closely matches the premium paid for the put, the net cost approaches zero. This is the defining characteristic of the strategy.

The result is a "collar" around the stock's value: gains are capped above the call strike, and losses are limited below the put strike. Between those two strikes, the investor participates fully in price moveme…

