Out-of-the-money puts cost money. Put options always carry a premium because they provide downside protection that buyers are willing to pay for. The further the put strike is below the current price, the cheaper the put but also the less protection it provides. A 5% out-of-the-money put is more expensive than a 10% OTM put but provides protection starting much closer to the current price.

Out-of-the-money calls generate premium. Selling a call option above the current price generates cash. The closer the call strike is to the current price, the more premium the call generates (because there is a higher probability of being exercised) but the more you limit potential upside.

The trade-off is explicit: to get a zero-cost collar where the call premium equals the put premium, you often have to sell a call with less upside room than you would ideally want, or accept a put strike further away than you would ideally want, or some combination of both. Understanding where you sit on that trade-off is the core design question in collar construction.
