may be too high a price to pay for that level of protection. Fund managers can offset the cost of buying put options by selling out-of-the-money calls against their portfolio. This strategy, known as a collar, provides downside protection below the strike level of the put, but limits upside return to the strike price of the call option (see figure 1c). Option strikes can be selected so that the premium received for the call completely offsets the premium paid for the put. In this case, the collar is said to be “costless”. To select the most suitable collar for a portfolio, the fund manager should evaluate how much protection the fund needs – and is willing to pay for – in relation to how much poten tial upside the fund can forego.

