### The Collar: Combining a Put With a Covered Call

A covered call involves selling a call option against owned stock to collect premium income upfront—but it caps potential gains if the stock rises above the strike price. 

- A collar combines a protective put with a covered call on the same stock—the premium from selling the call helps offset the cost of the put, in exchange for giving up some upside.
- When the premium collected equals the premium paid, it's called a zero-cost collar. Strikes don't need to sit equidistant from the stock price; a more bullish investor might choose a farther out-of-the-money call for more upside room.

