II.  Impact of CLL on S&P 500 Portfolio

Portfolio managers looking for ways to collar market risk will find  CBOE®s  S&P 500
95 -110 Collar Index (CLL) a convenient benchmark.  This collar overlays an SPX bear
spread (long put and short call) on the S&P 500, the accepted proxy for U.S. stock market
performance.  The  SPX  put  protects  an  investor  from  market  declines  and  the  SPX  call
helps finance the put.  As seen in Table 1 and Chart 1, a collar trades a ceiling on S&P
500  gains  for  a  floor  on  S&P  500  losses.    The  terms  of  this  exchange  vary  with  the
parameters of the collar, but the result is always a more compact distribution of returns
with shorter left and right tails, in other words, less risk and less return.

