Commonly, the put spread collar is structured in
such a way that all three legs of the position are
set to the same date of expiry. The option strikes
often are specified with a goal of neutralizing
the net premium flow (a so-called “zero-cost”
approach or, more accurately, a zero-premium
approach). Said another way, the puts are
generally specified in advance and the call strike
is set at a level that will exactly offset the net cost
of the put spread.

FRANK COOKE, CFA

