The hash rate ... hence the hash
...
The Hash Ribbons indicator gives a long-term “Bitcoin buy signal” over four stages:

- Miner capitulation (gray dot)
- Early miner recovery signs (green dots)
- End of miner capitulation (bright green dot)
- Final Bitcoin buy signal (blue dot).
...
First – Miner capitulation: Miners start selling more Bitcoin to cover their operating costs. Lower Bitcoin prices and/or higher electricity costs hurt miners’ profit margins, so more miners switch off their machines. That typically causes the hash rate to drop, with its 30-day moving average (bright green line) crossing below its 60-day moving average (gray line). This cross shows downward momentum for the hash rate and triggers the miner capitulation signal. After that, the chart’s red area shows miners are in the capitulation phase. And the 30-day moving average turns red, too.
...
Second – Early miner recovery signs: With the hash rate now lower after the miner capitulation signal, mining activity starts to pick up again. As a result, the hash rate starts to recover, which can trigger a few green dots to show up in the chart. But since the 30-day moving average is still below the 60-day moving average, miner capitulation isn’t officially over just yet.

