06FAQ
Fair questions, straight answers
Q1What are Hash Ribbons?
Charles Edwards' miner capitulation indicator. It compares the 30-day and 60-day moving averages of Bitcoin's hashrate, the total computing power securing the network. When the 30-day falls below the 60-day, miners are switching machines off: a capitulation.
Q2What is the Hash Ribbons buy signal?
The capitulation ends (the 30-day average climbs back above the 60-day) and price momentum turns up, measured as the 10-day average of price rising above the 20-day. Here only capitulations lasting at least 30 days count, because daily hashrate estimates are noisy.
Q3How accurate has it been?
It signalled in January 2019 at $3,632, July 2020, August 2021, August 2022 and January 2023 near the bear-market lows, and July 2024. It has also signalled several times through 2026 as mining margins tightened, so it marks miner stress, not only cycle bottoms.
Q4Where does the hashrate data come from?
Daily average hashrate from mempool.space, an open-source Bitcoin explorer, going back to the genesis block. Hashrate isn't measured directly; it's estimated from how fast blocks are found at the current difficulty.
