### The 1-3% rule

Risk no more than 1-3% of your total account value on any single options trade. This means maximum loss — not the amount invested, but the most you can lose if the trade goes completely wrong.

- For defined-risk trades (spreads, long options): maximum loss is the debit paid or the spread width minus credit. If your account is $50,000 and you cap risk at 2%, your maximum loss per trade is $1,000. A $5-wide bull put spread collecting $1.50 risks $3.50 per share ($350 per contract). You can trade 2 contracts ($700 max risk) and stay within the 2% limit.
- For undefined-risk trades (short strangles, naked options): maximum theoretical loss is unlimited or very large. Size these based on a realistic worst-case scenario, not the theoretical maximum. If a short strangle could reasonably lose $2,000-$3,000 in a sharp move (based on a 2-3 standard deviation stock move), that is your sizing input — not the theoretical unlimited loss.
...
- Long options (calls, puts): The entire premium is at risk. A $500 long call on a $50,000 account risks 1%. This feels small, but long options have a high loss rate — most expire worthless or are closed at a loss. Three or four simultaneous long option positions at 1% each is already 3-4% of the account at risk.
...
