Spread options strategies help investors shape a trade’s risk and reward with more precision than single-option strategies allow.  
Instead of buying or selling one option contract, a spread combines multiple contracts into one position. That structure can help  
traders reduce upfront cost, define maximum risk, cap potential losses, or target a specific price range.  
Spread strategies grew out of the basic building blocks of options trading: calls and puts. As listed options markets matured,  
traders began pairing long and short options with different strike prices to create more controlled outcomes. Over time, these  
pairings became standardized strategies with familiar names.  
The examples below use Nasdaq-100 Micro Index® Options (XND®), since its smaller notional size (1/100th that of the Nasdaq-100  
Index®, or NDX®) makes the math easier for examples relevant to individual investors. They assume a starting index level of 250  
for XND.  
Because every XND option controls 100 units worth of the underlying index, each point (dollar) is worth $100, just like in standard  
equity and ETF options. In other words, every XND option controls $25,000 worth of notional exposure to the Nasdaq-100 Index®.  
These examples are hypothetical and simplified. Actual option prices, taxes, commissions, spreads, and trading results will vary.  

