This paper examines systematic put-writing strategies applied to S&P 500 Index options, with a focus on position sizing as a key determinant of long-term performance. Despite the welldocumented volatility risk premium, where implied volatility generally exceeds realized volatility, the practical implementation of short-dated volatility-selling strategies remains underdeveloped in the literature. To address the non-linear, fat-tailed risks inherent in put-writing, this study introduces a novel algorithmic framework for dynamic position sizing — a hybrid architecture that integrates Monte Carlo-derived Kelly sizing with real-time VIX-regime scaling. Using at-the-money and out-of-the-money SPXW options with expirations ranging from 0 to 5 days, the analysis explores a broad design space, including moneyness levels, volatility estimators, and memory horizons. The results demonstrate that ultra-short-dated, far out-of-the-money options deliver superior risk-adjusted returns. The hybrid sizing method consistently balances return generation with robust drawdown control, particularly during low-volatility periods such as those observed in 2024. Ultimately, this research offers new insights into volatility harvesting through a novel adaptive sizing approach that responds to shifting market regimes. It provides practical guidance on constructing short-dated option strategies that can withstand diverse market environments. These findings have direct applications for institutional investors seeking to enhance portfolio efficiency through systematic exposure to volatility premia.
