Objective: Speculation  
Outlook: Moderately bullish  
A long call spread means buying one call option and selling another call option with a higher strike price and the same expiration  
date.  
A call option gives the buyer the right, but not the obligation, to benefit if the underlying asset rises above the strike price. In a long  
call spread, the trader buys a lower-strike call to gain upside exposure and sells a higher-strike call to help pay for it.  
This strategy developed as traders looked for a lower-cost alternative to buying calls outright. A long call can offer substantial  
upside, but it can also be expensive. Selling a higher-strike call reduces the cost of the trade, though it also limits the potential  
profit.  
A long call spread is typically used when a trader expects the underlying asset to rise but not necessarily surge far beyond a  
specific target.  
To execute a long call spread, an investor chooses an underlying asset, expiration date, and two call strike prices. They buy the  
lower-strike call, sell the higher-strike call, and pay a net debit, meaning the purchased option costs more than the sold option  
brings in.  
The maximum loss is the net debit paid. The maximum profit is the distance between the two strikes, minus the net debit.
